      </aside>

      <!-- Main Specification Document Sheet -->
      <article class="lg:col-span-9 min-w-0 os-window-lg bg-[#FAF8F2] p-6 sm:p-10 lg:p-12 spec-prose">
        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <h2 id="sec-1">1. Design Goals &amp; Overview</h2>

        <h3 id="sec-1-1">1.1 Design Goals</h3>
        <p>OmniBuf is designed around four core goals:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Stay Compatible with <code>.proto</code> File Semantics and Types:
            </strong>
            OmniBuf uses existing <code>.proto</code> definitions, field numbers (<code>1..N</code>
            ), scalar types, nested messages, repeated lists, and maps without requiring a new IDL,
            custom memory layouts, or manual alignment rules. Existing Protocol Buffers schemas and
            generated struct types can target the OmniBuf wire format directly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Faster Encoding and Decoding on Modern Hardware:</strong>
            Protocol Buffers (designed in 2001) relies on 7-bit LEB128 continuation loops (
            <code>0x80</code> branch per byte), two-pass <code>Size()</code> calculations for nested
            sub-messages, and byte-by-byte varint parsing when skipping fields. OmniBuf aligns the
            wire format with modern 64-bit little-endian CPUs:
            <ul>
              <li>
                Single-instruction trailing-zero count (<code>TZCNT</code> / <code>CTZ</code>)
                varints load 1, 2, 4, or 8 bytes in a single unaligned little-endian memory access.
              </li>
              <li>
                Single-pass forward encoding reserves and backpatches 2-byte length prefixes in
                place without a pre-pass.
              </li>
              <li>
                Explicit 4-bit wire types and outer container byte-length prefixes allow decoders to
                skip entire fields or nested containers in a single branchless <code>TZCNT</code>{' '}
                header hop without parsing inside strings, sub-messages, or arrays.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Schema-Less Value Decoding (with Optional Inline Field Names):</strong>
            In Protocol Buffers, Wire Type 2 (<code>LEN</code>) is overloaded for strings, raw
            bytes, nested messages, and packed repeated scalars, making it impossible to decode a
            packet without the <code>.proto</code> schema. OmniBuf uses explicit 4-bit wire types (
            <code>0..15</code>) so any decoder can losslessly inspect and decode every field's exact
            type and structure (<code>DecodeBlind</code>), including unsigned vs. ZigZag signed
            integers, fixed-width float arrays, and typed maps, with zero schema knowledge. When
            human-readable keys are desired on the wire, an optional inline schema sub-message (
            <code>Field #0</code>, <code>omnibuf.v1.SchemaMeta</code>) embeds field-number-keyed
            field names and nested child schemas at byte 0 of the root message or any nested
            sub-message.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Advanced Wire Techniques with a Flat Stream Layout:</strong>
            Rather than requiring vtables, footer offset tables, relative pointer graphs, or arena
            allocators, OmniBuf achieves single-pass encoding, fast zero-copy unmarshaling, raw byte
            concatenation (<code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>), and rapid header-hop field/array/map
            traversal using a single contiguous stream of <code>[Tag][Value]</code> fields with 4
            integer widths and 16 explicit wire types.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h3 id="sec-1-2">1.2 Core Wire Primitives</h3>
        <p>
          To achieve these goals, OmniBuf replaces Protobuf's Tag-Length-Value (TLV) + LEB128 with
          four wire primitives:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Branchless Prefix-Varints (<code>TZCNT</code>):
            </strong>{' '}
            The trailing zero bit count (<code>TZCNT</code>) of the first byte determines the total
            byte width (1, 2, 4, or 9 bytes) in little-endian order, eliminating byte-by-byte
            continuation-bit loops.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Explicit 4-Bit Wire Types (<code>0..15</code>):
            </strong>{' '}
            Every field tag encodes <code>(FieldNumber &lt;&lt; 4) | WireType</code>, unambiguously
            distinguishing unsigned integers (<code>WireUint</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>),
            ZigZag-encoded signed integers (<code>WireSint</code>, <code>WireSintArr</code>),
            strings, bytes, sub-messages, string arrays, length-prefixed sub-message arrays, typed
            maps, fixed-width floats/ints (and packed arrays <code>WireFixedArr</code>), zero-byte
            booleans (<code>true</code>/<code>false</code> encoded directly in the tag), and a
            length-prefixed extension escape hatch (<code>WireExtended = 15</code>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Single-Pass Overlong Length Backpatching:</strong> Variable-length containers (
            <code>WireSubMsg</code>, <code>WireStringArr</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>,{' '}
            <code>WireSintArr</code>, <code>WireSubMsgArr</code>, <code>WireMap</code>) reserve a
            2-byte Prefix-Varint length slot (<code>TZCNT = 1</code>) before writing their body in a
            single forward pass and backpatch the 14-bit length upon completion, eliminating
            two-pass <code>Size()</code> pre-computation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Concat-Compatible Field Stream with Outer Container Lengths:</strong> Because
            every scalar width is encoded in <code>b[0]</code> via <code>TZCNT</code> and every
            container begins with its total <code>ByteLen</code>, a decoder can skip any field in a
            single <code>O(1)</code> arithmetic jump without parsing inside sub-messages or arrays,
            and concatenating two encoded OmniBuf messages (<code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>)
            produces a valid merged OmniBuf message.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <!-- SECTION 2 -->
        <h2 id="sec-2">2. Message Packet Layout &amp; Concatenation Semantics</h2>
        <p>
          Every OmniBuf root message (and every nested sub-message in <code>WireSubMsg</code> or{' '}
          <code>WireSubMsgArr</code>) is a flat sequence of encoded <code>[Tag][Value]</code> fields
          with no header table or trailing footer:
        </p>

        <div class="my-4 p-4 bg-ink text-paper border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div class="text-paper/75 text-xs uppercase font-bold mb-2">
            Contiguous Packet Stream (0 .. N bytes)
          </div>
          <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-4 gap-2">
            <div class="p-2.5 bg-[#1F1F1F] border border-dashed border-acid/60 rounded">
              <div class="text-acid font-bold">Field #0 (opt)</div>
              <div class="text-paper/80 text-xs mt-0.5">[0x0B][SchemaMeta]</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-2.5 bg-[#1F1F1F] border border-paper/20 rounded">
              <div class="text-white font-bold">Field #f₁</div>
              <div class="text-paper/80 text-xs mt-0.5">[Tag][Value]</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-2.5 bg-[#1F1F1F] border border-paper/20 rounded">
              <div class="text-white font-bold">Field #f₂</div>
              <div class="text-paper/80 text-xs mt-0.5">[Tag][Value]</div>
            </div>
            <div class="p-2.5 bg-[#1F1F1F] border border-paper/20 rounded">
              <div class="text-white font-bold">... Field #fₖ</div>
              <div class="text-paper/80 text-xs mt-0.5">[Tag][Value]</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Field Ordering &amp; Raw Message Concatenation (<code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>):
            </strong>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Encoders SHOULD emit fields in ascending order of <code>FieldNumber</code> for
                deterministic output.
              </li>
              <li>
                Decoders MUST accept fields in any order and support duplicate field numbers so
                concatenating two encoded messages (<code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>) or appending
                an updated <code>[Tag][Value]</code> to the end of an existing packet follows
                Protocol Buffers merge semantics:
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <strong>Singular Scalars:</strong> Last occurrence wins (
                    <strong>"last write wins"</strong>).
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>Repeated Fields:</strong> Elements from later occurrences are appended
                    in order to earlier elements.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>
                      Nested Sub-Messages (<code>WireSubMsg</code>):
                    </strong>{' '}
                    Later occurrences merge recursively into the existing sub-message.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <strong>
                      Maps (<code>WireMap</code>):
                    </strong>{' '}
                    Entries from later occurrences merge into the map, with later keys overriding
                    earlier keys.
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Zero-Byte Empty Message:</strong> An empty message with no present fields is 0
            bytes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No Trailing Footer or Padding:</strong> The message ends immediately after the
            final byte of the last field.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <!-- SECTION 3 -->
        <h2 id="sec-3">
          3. Prefix-Varint Encoding (<code>TZCNT</code>)
        </h2>
        <p>
          All variable-length unsigned integers (field tags, <code>WireUint</code> values, container
          byte lengths, and element counts) use <strong>Prefix-Varints</strong>.
        </p>

        <h3 id="sec-3-1">3.1 Bit-Level Layout</h3>
        <p>
          The number of trailing zero bits (<code>TZCNT</code>) in Byte 0 (<code>b[0]</code>) tells
          the CPU the exact byte length of the integer (1, 2, 4, or 9 bytes) up front. The remaining
          high bits of the little-endian word store the unsigned value:
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>TZCNT</th>
                <th>Byte 0</th>
                <th>Width</th>
                <th>Value Range</th>
                <th>Little-Endian Encode / Decode</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">0</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">vvvvvvv1</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">1 B (7b)</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">0 .. 127</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">
                  <div>
                    <strong>Enc:</strong> uint8(v &lt;&lt; 1) | 0x01
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Dec:</strong> uint64(b[0] &gt;&gt; 1)
                  </div>
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">1</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">vvvvvv10</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">2 B (14b)</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">0 .. 16,383</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">
                  <div>
                    <strong>Enc:</strong> uint16(v &lt;&lt; 2) | 0x02
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Dec:</strong> uint64(LE16(b[0:2]) &gt;&gt; 2)
                  </div>
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">2</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">vvvvv100</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">4 B (29b)</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">0 .. 536,870,911</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">
                  <div>
                    <strong>Enc:</strong> uint32(v &lt;&lt; 3) | 0x04
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Dec:</strong> uint64(LE32(b[0:4]) &gt;&gt; 3)
                  </div>
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">&gt;= 3</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">00000000</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">9 B (64b)</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">
                  0 .. 2<sup>64</sup> - 1
                </td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">
                  <div>
                    <strong>Enc:</strong> b[0] = 0x00, LE64(b[1:9]) = v
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <strong>Dec:</strong> LE64(b[1:9])
                  </div>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 id="sec-3-2">3.2 Prefix-Varint Binary &amp; Hex Examples</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Value (v)</th>
                <th>TZCNT</th>
                <th>Width</th>
                <th>Formula</th>
                <th>Wire Binary (LE)</th>
                <th>Hex</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">0</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">0</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">1 B</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">(0 &lt;&lt; 1) | 1 = 1</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">00000001</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">01</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">127</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">0</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">1 B</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">(127 &lt;&lt; 1) | 1 = 255</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">11111111</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">ff</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">128</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">1</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">2 B</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">(128 &lt;&lt; 2) | 2 = 0x0202</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">00000010 00000010</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">02 02</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">300</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">1</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">2 B</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">(300 &lt;&lt; 2) | 2 = 0x04B2</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">10110010 00000100</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">b2 04</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">16,384</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">2</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">4 B</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">(16384 &lt;&lt; 3) | 4 = 0x00020004</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">00000100 00000000 00000010 00000000</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">04 00 02 00</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">
                  536,870,912 (2<sup>29</sup>)
                </td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">&gt;= 3</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">9 B</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">0x00 + LE64(2^29)</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">00000000 + 8B LE64</td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap font-bold">00 00 00 00 20 00 00 00 00</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="my-4 p-4 bg-white border-2 border-ink rounded-md text-sm sm:text-base">
          <div class="font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold uppercase text-ink/80 mb-1.5">
            Example: How 300 is encoded and decoded
          </div>
          <ol class="mb-0 space-y-1 text-sm sm:text-base">
            <li>
              <strong>Encode:</strong> <code>300</code> is between <code>128</code> and{' '}
              <code>16,383</code>, so it takes 2 bytes (<code>TZCNT = 1</code>). Shift{' '}
              <code>300</code> left by 2 bits and set the low 2 bits to <code>10</code> (
              <code>(300 &lt;&lt; 2) | 2 = 0x04B2</code>), then write it in little-endian order as{' '}
              <code>b2 04</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Decode:</strong> The first byte <code>0xB2</code> (<code>10110010</code>) ends
              with a single <code>0</code> bit (<code>TZCNT = 1</code>), telling the decoder to read
              2 bytes (<code>0x04B2</code>) and shift right by 2 bits (
              <code>0x04B2 &gt;&gt; 2 = 300</code>).
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <h3 id="sec-3-3">3.3 Overlong 2-Byte Prefix-Varints (Single-Pass Length Backpatching)</h3>
        <p>
          Decoders MUST dispatch strictly on <code>TZCNT(b[0])</code> without rejecting non-minimal
          ("overlong") encodings. Specifically, a length <code>v &lt; 128</code> encoded with the
          2-byte formula (<code>uint16(v &lt;&lt; 2) | 2</code>) is valid on the wire.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Fast Path (lengths up to 16,383 bytes):</strong> When encoding a container in a
            single forward pass, the encoder reserves a 2-byte slot at <code>lenPos</code>, writes
            the container body forward, and patches the 2-byte length slot at the end:{' '}
            <code>LE16(buf[lenPos:lenPos+2]) = (byteLen &lt;&lt; 2) | 2</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Large Container Expansion (&gt; 16,383 bytes):</strong> If a container exceeds
            16,383 bytes, the encoder shifts the container body right by 2 bytes (for a 4-byte
            varint up to 512 MiB) or 7 bytes (for a 9-byte varint) and writes the wider header.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Example (byteLen = 42 as 2-byte varint):</strong>{' '}
            <code>(42 &lt;&lt; 2) | 2 = 170 = 0x00AA</code> &rarr; hex <code>aa 00</code>. The
            decoder sees <code>TZCNT(0xAA) == 1</code> (2 bytes) and computes{' '}
            <code>0x00AA &gt;&gt; 2 = 42</code>.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <!-- SECTION 4 -->
        <h2 id="sec-4">
          4. Signed Integers (<code>ZigZag</code> Encoding, <code>WireSint = 12</code>)
        </h2>
        <p>
          Signed integers (<code>int32</code>, <code>sint32</code>, <code>int64</code>,{' '}
          <code>sint64</code>) map to unsigned <code>uint64</code> values via{' '}
          <strong>ZigZag encoding</strong> before being written as a <code>WireSint</code> (12)
          Prefix-Varint. Giving signed integers their own 4-bit wire type (
          <code>WireSint = 12</code>, distinct from <code>WireUint = 0</code>) lets schema-less
          decoders (<code>DecodeBlind</code>) decode negative and positive integers directly from
          raw bytes.
        </p>

        <h3 id="sec-4-1">4.1 ZigZag Formulas</h3>
        <div class="my-4 p-4 bg-ink text-paper border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed [&>pre]:!bg-transparent [&>pre]:!p-0 [&>pre]:!m-0 [&>pre]:!border-0">
