          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 id="sec-8-3">
          8.3 <code>repeated message</code> (<code>WireSubMsgArr</code>) Without Field-Name
          Duplication
        </h3>
        <p>
          In JSON, an array of 10,000 objects repeats the key strings <code>"city"</code> and{' '}
          <code>"rack"</code> 10,000 times. In OmniBuf, the parent message's <code>Field #0</code>{' '}
          places the child schema in <code>children[fieldNum]</code> <strong>once</strong>, and all
          10,000 elements in the <code>WireSubMsgArr</code> carry only compact numeric tags and
          values:
        </p>
        <pre><code><span class="text-paper/75">// 1. Stored ONCE in Parent Field #0:</span>
children[10] = &#123;
  type_name: "Neighbor",
  fields:    &#123; 1: "peer_asn", 2: "router", 3: "latency_us", 4: "established" &#125;
&#125;

<span class="text-paper/75">// 2. Data Field #10 (WireSubMsgArr, N = 10,000 elements):</span>
[ElemLen_0][Elem_0: compact tags + values, 0 repeated field names]
[ElemLen_1][Elem_1: compact tags + values, 0 repeated field names]
...</code></pre>

        <h3 id="sec-8-4">
          8.4 Raw Concatenation (<code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>) with <code>Field #0</code>
        </h3>
        <p>
          Because <code>Field #0</code> is a regular <code>WireSubMsg</code> (5) whose maps use{' '}
          <code>WireMap</code> (9), concatenating two self-describing packets (
          <code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>) works without special framing: when{' '}
          <code>DecodeBlind</code> sees a second <code>Field #0</code> later in the stream, it
          merges the new <code>SchemaMeta</code> into the active schema.
        </p>

