        <!-- SECTION 10 -->
        <h2 id="sec-10">10. Wire Validity &amp; Malformed Packet Rejection Rules</h2>
        <p>
          A conforming OmniBuf decoder MUST validate untrusted wire input and reject malformed or
          adversarial packets without panicking or reading out of bounds:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Empty Message:</strong> A 0-byte slice (<code>len(packet) == 0</code>) is a
            valid empty message with zero present fields.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>
              <code>Field #0</code> Wire Type &amp; Structure:
            </strong>{' '}
            Any occurrence of <code>Field #0</code> MUST have <code>WireType == WireSubMsg</code>{' '}
            (5) and encode a valid <code>omnibuf.v1.SchemaMeta</code> sub-message (where{' '}
            <code>fields</code> is a <code>WireMap</code> with <code>MapType = 0x03</code> and{' '}
            <code>children</code> is a <code>WireMap</code> with <code>MapType = 0x05</code>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Defined 4-Bit Wire Types:</strong> Wire types <code>0..14</code> are core wire
            types and <code>WireExtended</code> (15) is the length-prefixed extension escape hatch.
            Any tag whose payload violates its wire type's structural rules MUST be rejected.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Payload &amp; Container Bounds:</strong>
            <ul>
              <li>
                No Prefix-Varint or fixed-width value (<code>WireFixed32</code>,{' '}
                <code>WireFixed64</code>) may read past <code>len(packet)</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                Length-prefixed fields (<code>WireString</code>, <code>WireBytes</code>,{' '}
                <code>WireSubMsg</code>, <code>WireStringArr</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>,{' '}
                <code>WireSubMsgArr</code>, <code>WireMap</code>, <code>WireSintArr</code>,{' '}
                <code>WireFixedArr</code>, <code>WireExtended</code>) MUST satisfy{' '}
                <code>byteLen &lt;= uint64(len(packet) - pos)</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireStringArr</code>, <code>WireUintArr</code>, and <code>WireSintArr</code>{' '}
                elements MUST end on an exact element boundary at <code>pos + byteLen</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireFixedArr</code> MUST satisfy <code>byteLen &gt;= 1</code>, with{' '}
                <code>ElemType == WireFixed32</code> (10) and <code>(byteLen - 1) % 4 == 0</code>,
                or <code>ElemType == WireFixed64</code> (11) and <code>(byteLen - 1) % 8 == 0</code>
                .
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireExtended</code> MUST satisfy <code>byteLen &gt;= 1</code> (at least 1 byte
                for <code>ExtType</code>); schema-less decoders MUST reject unrecognized{' '}
                <code>ExtType</code> values, while schema-aware decoders skip unknown fields with{' '}
                <code>WireExtended</code> in <code>O(1)</code> time.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireSubMsgArr</code> elements MUST each satisfy{' '}
                <code>elemLen &lt;= uint64(len(arrPayload) - pos)</code> and the final element MUST
                end exactly at <code>len(arrPayload)</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code>WireMap</code> MUST satisfy <code>byteLen &gt;= 2</code>, contain a valid
                1-byte <code>MapType</code> header, satisfy{' '}
                <code>entryCount*2 &lt;= uint64(len(mapPayload) - pos)</code> before allocating
                entries (preventing OOM pre-allocation attacks), and the final entry MUST end
                exactly at <code>len(mapPayload)</code>. Duplicate keys follow "last write wins"
                semantics.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Resource &amp; Message Size Limits:</strong>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>
                  Maximum Nesting Depth (<code>MaxNestingDepth = 32</code>):
                </strong>{' '}
                By default, sub-message recursion deeper than 32 levels MUST be rejected.
                Implementations MAY provide an option to configure a custom recursion limit.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Message &amp; Container Byte-Size Limits:</strong> At the wire level,
                Prefix-Varints can represent container lengths up to{' '}
                <code>
                  2<sup>29</sup> - 1
                </code>{' '}
                bytes (~512 MiB) using a 4-byte header and up to{' '}
                <code>
                  2<sup>64</sup> - 1
                </code>{' '}
                bytes using a 9-byte header. To guard against memory exhaustion on untrusted inputs,
                implementations and transport layers MAY enforce configurable maximum message size
                limits:
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    RPC and framing layers MAY enforce a default per-message limit of 4 MiB (
                    <code>4,194,304</code> bytes, matching gRPC's default receive limit), and
                    general-purpose decoders MAY enforce a default total message limit of 64 MiB (
                    <code>67,108,864</code> bytes, matching Protocol Buffers' default limit) unless
                    overridden by the caller.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Implementations MAY reject individual container or string lengths exceeding{' '}
                    <code>536,870,911</code> bytes (the 4-byte Prefix-Varint maximum) unless
                    explicitly configured for &gt;512 MiB payloads.
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <!-- SECTION 11 -->
        <h2 id="sec-11">11. Language Implementation Requirements &amp; Conformance</h2>
        <p>
          OmniBuf implementations across programming languages SHOULD follow modern, idiomatic
          practices for their target ecosystem (such as zero-copy slice or span views where memory
          safety permits, buffer-append encoding APIs to support pooling, and standard language
          error handling). To provide a consistent developer experience across ecosystems, every
          complete OmniBuf implementation MUST support three ways to work with types and pass the
          full conformance test suite.
        </p>

        <h3 id="sec-11-1">11.1 Required Usage Modes</h3>
        <p>
          Every complete OmniBuf language implementation MUST support encoding and decoding in three
          ways:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Protobuf Compiler Plugin (<code>protoc-gen-omnibuf-*</code>):
            </strong>{' '}
            A dedicated <code>protoc</code> /{' '}
            <a href="https://buf.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              <code>buf</code>
            </a>{' '}
            plugin that generates reflection-free, single-pass forward encoders, branchless{' '}
            <code>TZCNT</code> decoders, and lazy header-hopping views (<code>Opaque</code>{' '}
            accessors and raw field splicing) directly from <code>.proto</code> schemas.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Usable on Protobuf Types Without the Plugin:</strong> Drop-in serialization and
            deserialization of standard <code>protoc</code>-generated message types (for example,{' '}
            <code>.pb.go</code> structs in Go or standard Protobuf classes in C++, Java, Python,
            Rust, and TypeScript) using the language's Protobuf runtime descriptors or reflection,
            requiring zero build-pipeline changes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Usable on Normal Classes, Objects, or Structs with Annotations (or Declarative Type
              Mapping):
            </strong>{' '}
            Direct serialization of plain language types (structs, classes, records, or data
            objects) without <code>.proto</code> files using native field annotations, attributes,
            decorators, or struct tags (such as Go's <code>`omnibuf:"1,name=city"`</code>, Rust
            derive attributes, or Java/C#/Python/TypeScript decorators) to declare field numbers,
            JSON/
            <code>Field #0</code> names, and wire-type overrides (<code>wire=sint</code>,{' '}
            <code>wire=fixed32</code>, <code>wire=fixed64</code>). In languages that do not have a
            built-in annotation or attribute feature, the implementation MUST provide an idiomatic
            declarative type-mapping API or descriptor table to bind field numbers, JSON names, and
            wire types to plain objects or structs.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h3 id="sec-11-2">11.2 Conformance Test Suite</h3>
        <p>
          All implementations MUST pass the official OmniBuf Conformance Test Suite across all
          supported usage modes. Modeled after Protocol Buffers' conformance harness, a dedicated
          conformance test runner binary executes a small adapter binary provided by each language
          implementation, feeding test requests and verifying responses to ensure:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Wire Round-Trip &amp; Interoperability:</strong> Deterministic decoding and
            re-encoding across all 16 wire types (<code>0..15</code>), single-byte and 2-byte field
            tags, overlong 2-byte container length prefixes, optional <code>Field #0</code>{' '}
            self-describing headers, raw concatenation merges, and schema-less inspection.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cross-Mode Equivalence:</strong> Identical wire semantics whether a message is
            processed via generated plugin code, standard Protobuf types without the plugin, or
            annotated/mapped native classes and structs.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Malformed Input Rejection:</strong> Strict, panic-free rejection of all invalid
            or adversarial wire packets defined in <a href="#sec-10">Section 10</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>

